# Agent Hooks

## Architecture

Rule Fairy is one plugin for both harnesses. Claude Code reads `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` and `hooks/hooks.json`;
Codex reads the portable root `plugin.json` (Agent Plugins schema, which makes it discover `skills/` itself) with
`.codex-plugin/plugin.json` as its overlay pointing at `hooks/codex-hooks.json`. Every registered command runs
`hooks/run <harness>/<script>.bb`, a POSIX shell wrapper that resolves the plugin root (`CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT`,
`PLUGIN_ROOT`, or its own location), adds the usual `bb` install locations to a minimal PATH, and starts Babashka
with `--config <plugin>/bb.edn` so a consuming repository's `bb.edn` never reaches the classpath.

Rule discovery, frontmatter parsing, glob and prompt matching, import expansion and rendering live in `rule-fairy.rules`,
on top of the line-level Markdown model in `rule-fairy.markdown`; `rule-fairy.config` reads `rule-fairy.edn`;
incremental transcript scanning is `rule-fairy.transcript`. The rules source is `rules/project-rules-source`: Cursor's
`.cursor/rules/**/*.mdc` unless `rule-fairy.edn` at the project root sets `{:rules {:dirs [...] :extensions [...]}}`. Rule
names stay relative to their own directory; a name present in two directories fails the index.
Session dedup state is `rule-fairy.session-state`, shared by both adapters. The adapters own everything harness-specific:
hook input, project-root discovery, transcript location and marker pattern, glob caches, path relativisation, and output
encoding.

The review side is separate from the hooks. `rule-fairy.bundle` builds a review bundle for a path list from
`rule-fairy.instructions` (harness instruction files, imports, one copy per real file), the rules and their documentation,
and `rule-fairy.dedup` (repeated sections and long paragraphs replaced by a line naming the kept copy), and writes it under
the self-ignored `.rule-fairy/review/<mode>/`. `rule-fairy.plan` extracts the paths a plan intends to touch and
`rule-fairy.git` records revision facts. The skills `skills/review` and `skills/review-plan` drive that through a
`scripts/run` shim per skill, the same `--config` trick as `hooks/run`; `agents/rules-reviewer.md` is the read-only reviewer
Claude Code runs for them, and Codex follows the shared `skills/review/references/procedure.md` inline.

### Codex

| Hook | Event | Script | Purpose |
|------|-------|--------|---------|
| `hooks/codex/prompt.bb` | `UserPromptSubmit` | Always-on rules plus keyword match on prompt text | Inject rules when the user mentions relevant topics |
| `hooks/codex/post_edit.bb` | `PostToolUse` (edits) | Glob match on edited file path | Inject rules after an edit so the next pass can revise if needed |

Both entries set `additionalContextLimit: 0`; see Codex bounded delivery. Codex runs a hook in the session's working
directory and exports no project directory, so `hooks/codex/common.bb` takes the project root from
`RULE_FAIRY_PROJECT_DIR` or else finds it from the working directory: the nearest directory, up to the enclosing
checkout root, that holds a `rule-fairy.edn` or the default rules directory, else that checkout root, else the
working directory itself outside git. A session started in a repository subdirectory with its own rules is that
subdirectory's project, and a hook run deeper down still finds it. No git process runs for that, and the
registration command is the bare hook runner. Codex loads plugin hooks after the user trusts them; use `/hooks` to
review.

### Claude Code

| Hook | Event | Script | Purpose |
|------|-------|--------|---------|
| `hooks/claude/prompt.bb` | `UserPromptSubmit` | Always-on rules plus keyword match on prompt text | Inject rules when the user mentions relevant topics |
| `hooks/claude/edit.bb` | `PreToolUse` (Edit, Write, MultiEdit, NotebookEdit, MCP edit tools) | Glob match on target file path | Inject rules before a matching file is edited |

Claude Code runs hook commands in the session `cwd`, which follows the agent's shell, and exports
`CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR`, `CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT`, and `CLAUDE_PLUGIN_DATA`. `hooks/claude/common.bb` takes the project root from
`CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR`; nothing depends on the cwd.

Each Rule Fairy event has 12 commands for the same script. `RULE_FAIRY_LANE` gives every command a distinct identity because Claude deduplicates
identical command hooks. Lane `n` owns frame `n` in the shared event batch.

## Injection Flow

### Prompt-based
1. Receives user prompt text
2. Indexes the frontmatter of every rule in the project's rules source (default `.cursor/rules/**/*.mdc`; `rule-fairy.edn` can
   move it)
3. Selects every rule with `alwaysApply: true`, plus every rule where all `promptRequires` terms and at least one
   `promptAnyOf` term match the prompt
4. Injects the selected contents, labelled `[rule-fairy matched: alwaysApply ...]` and `[rule-fairy matched: keyword ...]`

Always-on rules are injected with the first prompt of a session, not at session start, and again after compaction or
transcript growth like any other rule. Because they match every prompt, the session state is consulted on every prompt;
the hooks then read transcript metrics once per prompt event to decide on reinjection. Do not duplicate always-on rule
content in `CLAUDE.md` or `AGENTS.md`.

### Glob-based
1. Receives paths from tool input (`file_path`, `path`, or apply-patch content)
2. Parses `globs:` from the rules source's frontmatter, through a per-harness glob cache that is invalidated by rule-tree mtime
   and by a changed rules source
3. Matches file path against parsed globs
4. Injects matched MDC contents via JSON `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`

### Claude bounded delivery
Claude Code saves a hook output larger than 10,000 characters to a temporary file and gives the model only a preview and the file path. Agents do not reliably read that file.

The Claude hooks therefore deliver one logical injection through 12 fixed output lanes:

1. The first lane to acquire the per-session file lock selects the rules, reads transcript metrics once, and creates the event batch.
2. The batch renders the matched rules once and splits semantic rule and documentation blocks at Markdown headings or paragraphs.
3. Fenced code blocks stay intact. A fenced block that cannot fit fails instead of producing invalid Markdown.
4. The batch packs the parts into context frames of at most 9,000 characters. Only the first frame carries the full matched-rule roster
   and hook description; later frames use that space for rule content.
5. Lane `n` writes and flushes frame `n` when it exists, then acknowledges delivery.
6. The final acknowledgement marks all rules as injected and replaces the batch with a 30-second completion marker so late handlers cannot recreate the event.

Frame order is not significant. Every frame has a shard marker, and every split part repeats its rule or documentation heading and includes a part number.

A repeated event retries the frame for an unacknowledged lane. If one lane fails, the batch remains uncommitted so the next matching event
re-injects the full rule set. If a batch needs more frames than configured lanes, lane `0` reports the capacity error, no rule content is
delivered, and no rule is marked as injected. An event with no rules to inject stores the same short-lived completion marker so the remaining
lanes do not repeat rule selection or transcript scanning.

### Codex bounded delivery
Codex applies its own cap to hook `additionalContext`: 2,500 tokens by default. Larger output is saved under
`<temp_dir>/hook_outputs/<session_id>/<uuid>.txt` and the model receives a head-and-tail preview with the path, so a rule
bundle is silently cut in the same way Claude cuts output over 10,000 characters.

Unlike Claude, the Codex cap is configurable per hook. Every Rule Fairy hook entry in `hooks/codex-hooks.json` sets
`additionalContextLimit: 0`, which passes the complete `additionalContext` through. The Codex adapter therefore delivers
one unsharded payload and relies on the engine's 64 KiB documentation budget as the only size guard. Do not remove the
key when editing the registration; the failure is silent.

Codex states that the setting applies only to `additionalContext` and that tool feedback and continuation prompts keep the
default limit. Whether the `continue: false` path used by `hooks/codex/post_edit.bb` counts as feedback is not documented.

### Required documentation
Both paths render their payload through `rule-fairy.rules`:

1. Collect standalone `@path` and `@path#heading-fragment` directives outside fenced code blocks, leaving a line in the
   body that names the reference where each stood, since the section renders after the rule bodies, possibly in another shard
2. Resolve each path from the repository root and reject paths that escape it
3. Select whole files or heading-bounded Markdown sections
4. Merge overlapping source ranges across all matched rules, keeping which rules import each range; the block's heading names them, so a reader can tell which rule, and so which files, a section governs
5. Remove non-nested `<!-- agent-context: omit -->` blocks
6. Append the selected canonical documentation to the rule payload

The expanded documentation has a 64 KiB safety budget per hook payload. Missing files, missing headings, invalid omission markers, and oversized payloads fail instead of silently dropping required context.

## Dedup / Reinjection

Hooks share the same dedup mechanism to avoid flooding the context:

- **Codex state files** in `.rule-fairy/codex/<session-id>.edn`
- Both state directories write a `.gitignore` containing `*` on first use, so they never show up as untracked
- **Claude state files** in `.rule-fairy/claude/<session-id>.edn`
- **Claude in-flight batches** in `.rule-fairy/claude/batches/<session-id>/`
- Tracks `{rule-name → {:size transcript-size-at-injection :compaction-count n}}` in one `:sizes` map per session. Every
  hook of a harness shares it, so a rule delivered by the prompt hook is not delivered again by the edit hook, and the
  other way round.
- `:transcript-scan {:scanned-bytes n :compaction-count c}` remembers how far the transcript has been counted. Each event
  reads only the bytes appended since, and only complete lines, so a record is never split across two scans. A transcript
  shorter than the cursor is rescanned from the start. Shared implementation: `rule-fairy.transcript`.
- First injection: always inject, except that a session whose transcript already holds Rule Fairy injections it has no
  record of (a forked Claude Code session replays its parent's hook records under its own session id) starts with those
  rules marked as injected, read once from the hook_success attachments after the last compaction summary; a frame
  counts only when its output opens with the shard header, and a delivery only when all its shards, grouped by the
  id in the header, are present
  (`rule-fairy.transcript/inherited-injections`)
- Subsequent: re-inject after context compaction or **2MB of transcript growth** since last injection. Transcript growth is a deliberately
  coarse fallback for silent context cleanup that can discard earlier hook injections without a normal compaction record.
- Existing numeric state entries are treated as pre-compaction metrics and migrate on their next injection
- Old state files cleaned up after 4 hours

### Why per-session files (not a shared file)
Multiple Claude Code sessions run concurrently (different terminals, Cursor + CLI, etc.). A shared state file with read-modify-write has a
race condition: session A reads, session B reads, A writes, B writes, and A's dedup tracking is lost. Per-session files eliminate this.

## Known Pitfalls

### Subagents run the same hooks
Claude Code fires the tool hooks inside subagents too, with `agent_id` and `agent_type` in the input, and the state is
keyed by session, so a rule injected into a subagent would be recorded as delivered and withheld from the main
thread afterwards. The Claude hooks therefore do nothing when `agent_id` is present (`rule-common/main-thread?`).
Codex marks nothing on a subagent's tool events and gives them the parent's session id, so the same confusion stands
there and cannot be detected; the README lists it as a gap. Injecting into subagents with a history of their own is a
possible follow-up.

### Hooks run in the session `cwd`, which follows the Bash tool's shell
Claude Code spawns hook commands in the session's current working directory, and that directory moves when the agent's
Bash tool runs `cd`. Codex runs hooks in the session cwd too. Nothing in the hooks may resolve a relative path against
the cwd: the Claude adapter takes the project root from `CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR`, the Codex adapter walks up from the cwd
to the nearest `.git` entry, and the wrapper passes the plugin's `bb.edn` explicitly. Running sessions capture hook config at
startup; after installing or updating the plugin, restart the session or review `/hooks`.

### Claude `transcript_path` from hook input is unreliable
Claude Code bugs (#3046, #8710, #13668) cause `transcript_path` to be stale, wrong, or point to non-existent files after `/clear`,
compaction, or in long sessions. The hooks locate the transcript by its globally unique `session_id` under the `projects/` directory of
Claude's configuration directory (`~/.claude`, or `$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` when set) instead of trusting `transcript_path` or reproducing
Claude's undocumented project-directory encoding. Don't "simplify" this back to using the provided path.

Codex hook input includes `transcript_path`; Codex hooks may use it. Keep the Claude workaround scoped to `hooks/claude`.

### File edits can target another checkout, and the project need not be the checkout root
Sessions edit absolute paths in linked worktrees, which Claude Code keeps under `.claude/worktrees/` inside the
repository, and a session may start in a subdirectory of its repository. Relativizing an edited path against the
session project then yields `.claude/worktrees/w/src/a.clj` or `backend/src/a.clj`, and rooted globs such as
`src/**` miss it. Both adapters match through `rule-fairy.git/rule-path`: the path relative to the checkout enclosing
the edited file, less the project's own position inside its checkout.

### Edits made through the shell are detected after the command, not before
The edit hooks see only the paths a tool input names. A file written by a Bash command (a heredoc, `sed -i`, `tee`, `mv`,
an interpreter one-liner) reaches no edit hook, and Claude Code's auto mode nudges the model towards exactly that: in
auto-mode transcripts at least one source edit in five went through the shell. The shell hook (`hooks/claude/shell.bb`
on `PostToolUse` and `PostToolUseFailure` for `Bash`; `hooks/codex/post_edit.bb` when `tool_name` is `Bash`, which is
the name both harnesses give the shell tool) closes that gap after the fact: `rule-fairy.changes` lists the `git status`
entries whose modification or inode change time is at or after the session's last shell check, plus the files the
commits since that check's HEAD touched, and the matching rules are injected with the post-edit contract. The check is
the marker the prompt hook starts and every shell check advances; paths stay pending in the session state until their
rules were delivered (on Claude once every lane of the batch has acknowledged its frame, on Codex once the injection is
prepared) and are otherwise retried by the next shell command, so a broken rule or a delivery cut short is retried
rather than silently losing the edit, a rule set too large for the lanes arrives across successive commands, and a
single rule too large for the lanes fails loudly on every command until it is fixed. Consequences to keep in mind: the rules arrive one model cycle after the
write, never before it; a moved file counts through its inode change time, since `mv` keeps the modification time; times
compare at whole seconds so a coarse filesystem cannot hide an edit; a write by another session between two checks is
attributed to this one and injects its rules once; ignored files and deletions are never reported; and outside a git
checkout nothing is detected. The check is switched per harness by `:shell-hook` in `rule-fairy.edn`, on for Claude
Code and off for Codex by default, because Codex makes many shell calls and rarely edits through them. Codex runs
`PostToolUse` after a command that exits non-zero as well, with no separate failure event and no exit code in the
input, so where the hook is on a write made before the failure is seen. `FileChanged` was not used because it delivers no context to the model and
watches literal filenames only; a `Stop` safety net is unnecessary because context injected after the last shell call
of a turn already reaches the model on that turn.

### Migrating from project-local hooks
A repository that registered the original scripts in `.claude/settings.json` or `.codex/hooks.json` must remove those
entries when the plugin is installed, or every rule is injected twice. To try a checkout of this repository as the plugin,
add it as a local marketplace (`claude plugin marketplace add ./rule-fairy`) rather than pointing settings at its scripts.

### Nil transcript size breaks dedup
When the transcript file doesn't exist yet (new session, test environment), `transcript-size` returns nil. If nil gets stored as the size,
`needs-reinjection?` returns true on every subsequent call. The fix: `mark-injected!` stores `(or current-size 0)` to ensure a numeric value.

### `needs-reinjection?` threshold direction
An earlier version checked `(< current-size stored-size)` to detect transcript shrinkage, but transcripts only grow. The correct check is
`(>= (- current-size stored-size) threshold)` to re-inject after significant growth (2MB).

### Context compaction does not shrink transcripts
Codex records compaction in the transcript while the transcript file continues growing. Dedup therefore tracks the count of top-level
`compacted` records and re-injects a matching rule after that count changes. Transcript size alone cannot detect context eviction.
Claude transcripts expose compactions as `"isCompactSummary":true`; the Claude hooks track the count of those records equivalently.
Claude can also clear older context without persisting a usable compaction boundary. Keep the 2MB transcript-growth fallback unless Claude
exposes a reliable signal for that cleanup in the session transcript.

### Concurrent hook handlers and sessions
The original design used a single `.mdc-injected-session.edn` for all sessions. Concurrent sessions (multiple terminals, Cursor + CLI) would
race on read-modify-write, losing each other's dedup tracking. Fixed by switching to per-session files in `.rule-fairy/claude/`.

Claude's Rule Fairy lanes also run concurrently inside one session. Batch creation, frame lookup, acknowledgements, and the final dedup update use a
per-session file lock. Only the lane that creates a batch may read transcript metrics, and that read persists the scan cursor, so it must
stay under the lock. Do not move those operations back into the event scripts or update session state from an individual frame.

Codex launches every hook matching an event concurrently, and a subagent's hooks carry the parent's session id, so
its hooks contend for one state file too. Each Codex hook therefore reads the state, decides what to inject and
records it inside one `with-session-lock` call, the same per-session file lock (`rule-fairy.session-state/with-lock`)
the Claude lanes use, and prints its output after the lock is released. A helper that loads or saves session state
outside that call reintroduces the lost update.

The glob cache is shared by every session of a project while the locks are per session, so it, like the session
and batch files, is written through `rule-fairy.session-state/replace-file!`: a temporary file beside the target
moved into place atomically. A plain truncating write lets a concurrent reader see partial EDN and fail its hook.

The lanes' outputs land in the transcript in arrival order, not lane order, so a rule split across shards can read out of
sequence; the `[rule-fairy shard n/m <delivery>]` labels are what lets the model reassemble it, and the delivery id is
what lets the fork reader tell neighbouring deliveries apart. Anything that must read in order
belongs in one shard, and per-lane commentary would either repeat on every lane or arrive scrambled. This has not caused
trouble so far, and whether Claude guarantees any ordering for the hooks of one event is unchecked.

### Glob cache staleness
The MDC glob cache is invalidated when an `.mdc` file or any directory in the rules tree is newer than the cache. Directory mtimes ensure
that additions, removals, and renames invalidate nested rule paths. Claude's file hook trusts caches younger than 30 seconds before it runs
this check. A content change also refreshes the cache when the globs do not change. This extra refresh is not harmful.

### Catch-all globs fire on every edit
A rule whose glob covers a whole language (`**/*.clj, **/*.cljs, **/*.cljc, **/*.edn`) is injected on the first edit of any
such file in every session. That is right for conventions that apply everywhere, but it makes that rule the most frequently
injected one. If it is too noisy, narrow the glob.

### Output format differs between agents and events
- Codex hooks output JSON. Use `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` for reminders.
- `hooks/claude/prompt.bb` (UserPromptSubmit): prints plain text to stdout
- `hooks/claude/edit.bb` (PreToolUse): prints JSON with `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`

Claude Code treats hook output differently per event type. Plain text becomes passive context; JSON enables structured control
(`additionalContext`, `permissionDecision`, `updatedInput`). Don't make the Claude scripts match Codex without testing Claude behavior.

Both Claude event types use the same batch protocol despite their different output encodings. Their commands must use the same script with
distinct, contiguous `RULE_FAIRY_LANE` values from `0` through `11`. `rule_common_test.bb` checks the identities and lane set.

### Testing hooks locally
`bb test` runs every suite, including install tests that execute the registered commands from `hooks/hooks.json` and
`hooks/codex-hooks.json` through a shell against a temporary project. To run one hook by hand against a project:

```bash
# Claude prompt hook, lane 0, project root from CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR
echo '{"prompt":"fix the deftest","session_id":"test"}' \
  | CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT="$PWD" CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR=/path/to/project RULE_FAIRY_LANE=0 hooks/run claude/prompt.bb

# Claude edit hook, lane 0
echo '{"session_id":"test","tool_input":{"file_path":"/path/to/project/src/file.clj"}}' \
  | CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT="$PWD" CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR=/path/to/project RULE_FAIRY_LANE=0 hooks/run claude/edit.bb

# Codex prompt and post-edit hooks, run from the project root
(cd /path/to/project && echo '{"prompt":"fix the deftest","session_id":"test","cwd":"'"$PWD"'"}' \
  | PLUGIN_ROOT=/path/to/rule-fairy /path/to/rule-fairy/hooks/run codex/prompt.bb)
(cd /path/to/project && echo '{"session_id":"test","cwd":"'"$PWD"'","tool_input":{"file_path":"src/file.clj"}}' \
  | PLUGIN_ROOT=/path/to/rule-fairy /path/to/rule-fairy/hooks/run codex/post_edit.bb)
```

A single Claude lane prints one frame; complete multi-lane delivery and dedup are exercised by `bb test`.

## Adding a New Rule

1. Create the `.mdc` file in `.cursor/rules/` with frontmatter globs
2. Add `promptAnyOf` and optional `promptRequires` frontmatter if prompt-based injection is wanted
3. The glob-based hook picks up new `.mdc` files automatically (cache refreshes on mtime change)
4. Update project guidance references if the rule should be discoverable outside hooks
